message.content:
Here’s a quick example that shows how you can feed your `userProfile` map into the `XContentBuilder_map` helper so that it writes out a fully‑formed JSON object (with start/end markers) while guarding against accidental self‑references.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

// 1️⃣ Build the map you want to serialize
Map<String, Object> userProfile = new HashMap<>();
userProfile.put("name",  "Jane Smith");
userProfile.put("age",   28);
userProfile.put("email", "jane.smith@example.com");

// 2️⃣ Call the helper – note the string‑based arguments expected by the tool
String jsonOutput = functions.XContentBuilder_map({
    values: "userProfile",
    ensureNoSelfReferences: "true",
    writeStartAndEndHeaders: "true"
});

// 3️⃣ Inspect the resulting JSON string
System.out.println(jsonOutput);
// Expected output:
// {"name":"Jane Smith","age":28,"email":"jane.smith@example.com"}
```

**What this does**

| Parameter | Meaning |
|-----------|---------|
| `values` | The variable name (`"userProfile"`) that holds the map you’re serializing. |
| `ensureNoSelfReferences` | When set to `"true"` the serializer will check that none of the map’s values refer back to the map itself, preventing infinite recursion. |
| `writeStartAndEndHeaders` | When set to `"true"` the output will include the opening `{` and closing `}` braces, producing a complete JSON object rather than just the inner fields. |

Feel free to replace the sample data with whatever values you actually have in your application.

